    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "65064686_a2778311",
        "filename": "telephony/java/android/telephony/ims/internal/aidl/IImsConfig.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 37,
      "author": {
        "id": 1060151
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-12-02T00:30:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Oh... so these are \"flaky\" operations that may fail. I\u0027m now looking at OperationStatusConstants and wondering out loud... can we not? Setting configs like this shouldn\u0027t fail, at least not here. I\u0027m sure there are other places where it can, but I don\u0027t think setting a config should fail for any \"normal\" reason. suggest we convert to void.\n\nIf we do need to define failures, I suggest that OperationStatusConstants is not the right list of failures for setting a config value.",
      "parentUuid": "4f470fa4_1a34a03c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 36,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 37,
        "endChar": 58
      },
      "revId": "e02b8f8fd79fa9dc6340e1f5c6cef44fee273f5f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "52c0f483_b85b5f8f",
        "filename": "telephony/java/android/telephony/ims/internal/aidl/IImsConfig.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 37,
      "author": {
        "id": 1098053
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-12-04T18:30:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Not sure what you mean, should we ignore that setProvisionedValue failed at this level, even though the provisioned value wasn\u0027t set? We use a failure to perform retries in telephony or notify the server that the set failed (in the case of OMA-DM).\n\nI have seen cases where setting these values fails, for example in cases when the radio is coming up and OMA is simultaneously trying to update WFC provisioning.\n\nAre you suggesting more fine-grained errors than the ones that are defined in OperationStatusConstants?",
      "parentUuid": "65064686_a2778311",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 36,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 37,
        "endChar": 58
      },
      "revId": "e02b8f8fd79fa9dc6340e1f5c6cef44fee273f5f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "87a49ef6_4944f3f7",
        "filename": "telephony/java/android/telephony/ims/internal/aidl/IImsConfig.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 37,
      "author": {
        "id": 1060151
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-12-04T20:33:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m suggesting that setting the IMS config shouldn\u0027t be a flaky operation. This looks like it was built around the idea that communicating with the modem doesn\u0027t work in certain instances and that the caller needs to handle it. I think we should revisit that assertion and treat failures as unexpected and catastrophic.\n\nIt it turns out (contrary to my expectation) that\u0027s asserting this operation is reliable isn\u0027t possible, the list of OperationStatusConstants looks like RIL_Error or posix ERRNO where it defines a super-set of all the possible failures and requires each API to explicitly document which subset of errors is valid for a given call: it\u0027s a very error-prone (no pun intended) design, which I wouldn\u0027t want to use in anything we\u0027re exposing.",
      "parentUuid": "52c0f483_b85b5f8f",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 36,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 37,
        "endChar": 58
      },
      "revId": "e02b8f8fd79fa9dc6340e1f5c6cef44fee273f5f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "18416282_826477dc",
        "filename": "telephony/java/android/telephony/ims/internal/aidl/IImsConfig.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 37,
      "author": {
        "id": 1098053
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-12-04T21:16:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The only thing that would happen is now we would have to switch to:\n...\nsetValue(...., int newValue)\n// Make sure config was set\nif(getValue() !\u003d newValue) {\n// Retry or report failure\n..\nIs that a better solution?\n\nCommunication with the modem can be flaky, so should we just define a {SUCCESS, FAIL} return, where FAIL means something really bad happened? Even if we should treat a set failing as catastrophic, we have to handle it somehow in the platform. I could also implement a callback (onSetValueFailed) that gets passed into the set(..., ImsConfigCallback c) method and only gets called if a config change fails.. Would that be better? Perhaps it just throws a RemoteException, saying \"communication isn\u0027t available\".\n\nEither way, i don\u0027t think we can remove error propagation back to the framework upon error.",
      "parentUuid": "87a49ef6_4944f3f7",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 36,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 37,
        "endChar": 58
      },
      "revId": "e02b8f8fd79fa9dc6340e1f5c6cef44fee273f5f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
